n mind as you're watching this and if  you want a full breakdown of the  differences like of the project types  and the design process and the education  as well as the skills and personality  that's required to thrive in each career  check out the video right here i think  there's a lot of nuances to the way we  approach problems that's quite different  when it came to developing the concept  and i really try to distill it down to  the essence i think the biggest  difference is that architects derive  their concepts from external sources  and interior designers derive their  sources from internal sources let me  explain a concept is an idea that  becomes a foundation and identity of  every design project and it drives it  forward it's the idea that informs the  different aspects of your building in  every step of the design process and  whenever you're stuck you can refer back  to the concept to get some clarity in  your direction it's also a really good  way of embedding meaning into the  project that's beyond just the building  or the space itself but most importantly  it's a tool to get buy-in from clients  and your team and get everyone aligned  on the same page each office and each  designer is unique in their way of  developing a concept so try to learn  from others and as you work in different  offices try to also develop your own  methods  architects tend to derive their concepts  from external sources when you see an  architect presenting their work we  typically start off by describing the  context or the problems or opportunities  they see on the site or the program and  this is all of your physical or social  constraints of your project and when you  actually look at the most convincing  presenters like bjr gangels they distill  the problem into a really clear series  of diagrams or images and they use this  to develop their functional or formal or  material strategy which forms the basis  for their concept but this isn't the  case with just large buildings even when  architects are operating at a much  smaller scale like interior renovations  the way we present concepts is almost  always dependent on an external or  site-specific or program-specific source  let me just pause for a quick moment for  a message from our sponsor soundcore i  don't know about you guys but i feel  like i'm constantly surrounded by noise  and distractions and for me having a  device that can help me stay focused  really makes a difference soundcore  space a40 offers the perfect balance  between noise cancelling performance  sound quality and battery life as a  creator  i always try to absorb content as much  as possible so listening to audiobooks  whenever i can now is kind of a part of  my daily routine  the space a40 adaptive noise cancelling  feature automatically detects different  types of background noise and improves  word comprehension even at really high  reading speeds the dedicated soundcore  app lets you customize the noise  canceling parameters and and you can  even create a tailored sound experience  via sound id  which is a feature that generates an eq  profile based on your personal hearing  biometrics it creates a really rich and  vibrant sound experience that really  makes me wanna listen to music even more  plus these earbuds are really  lightweight and comfy to wear and they  also have really useful magnets that can  keep them together inside or even  outside of their charging box and with  one single charge you can get up to 10  hours of playing time check the link in  the description if you want to know more  i'll give you an example with the  project that i'm working on right now  which is the library renovation where  the driving concept is this idea of the  urban courtyard this was from a series  of contextual factors the library has  this really beautiful  garden that already kind of functions as  a central green space in this very dense  neighborhood and you can see this solid  void relationship within that building  as well when you enter the building  you've got this big three-story atrium  and the library is also in a really  diverse neighborhood it's got a really  high percentage of immigrants and people  whose first language is not english the  functional program of the library is  also pretty diverse we're putting in a  digital media lab a lounge a recording  studio a grab-and-go section so we  developed this idea of the urban  courtyard  [Music]  and courtyards have been pretty  universal across different cultures  throughout history and although it first  came to place to control the  microclimatic conditions within  buildings over time it evolved to become  a social gathering place and so this  shared architectural language of the  courtyard brings these diverse cultures  and programs together while also  bringing in an element of nature you can  see how we developed the concept by  tying it to some of these external  conditions the broader and more flexible  your concept is the better because you  can tie it to a lot of different aspects  of the design for example the courtyard  this can be functional or formal or  social or organizational and it has a  lot of visual associations so it can be  also used to develop your material  strategy but a concept like let's say  a digital hub which is an idea that  we've been talking a lot about it  doesn't have a lot of historical or  architectural or visual associations so  it's a little bit harder to use it as a  concept even the most form-driven  architecture like frank gehry's work is  in some way relating to its context now  architects will take this and some  architects will create a mood board  which i'll go into in a little bit but  when it comes to the hierarchy of  importance for architects like based on  our training and our design philosophy  it's function  organization order  and then we have mood  for interior designers they don't really  have these complex site conditions to  work with right so they derive a lot of  their concept ideas from internal  sources and what i found is that the  concept development process in interior  design  is a lot more intimately connected to  the client  and their visions for the project and so  during this phase we just sit with the  client and we just talk about their  vision for the space not just  programmatically or functionally but how  they want the space to feel  and what's the image they want to  project and the client they can bring  their own inspiration images  and if it's like a commercial client  they can bring their own branding  guidelines and we basically work with  the client to put together visual  representation of this feeling which is  called a mood board but before doing  this we would  write a very small paragraph  or like a list of words  or a poem that kind of captures the mood  or the direction that we got from the  client and i found this process super  interesting for me language is not  really my strong suit so this was  definitely kind of hard for me but  first of all like it's a really really  great way to consolidate all the  information that you got from the client  but you're not just reciting what the  client is saying you're adding and  you're elevating all the things that  they said  into a language that have a visual  connection so for example we were  designing an office for a construction  company and some of the words that they  used was things like trustworthy  kind family business and so you could  use that to say something like grounded  and family roots and this kind of  descriptive writing it works hand in  hand with the mood board which is  basically a collection of images that  represent the mood or the feeling that  you're trying to create these images  aren't necessarily precedent images  they're literally like images that  combine together to create a mood  everyone probably has a slightly  different way of doing it but the way  that i was taught you shouldn't actually  include images of actual building  elements that actually comes in a little  bit later in the design process i've  seen a lot of different reactions to  this concept development process some  clients are a little bit more literal  and more pragmatic and they just want to  quickly move on to the actual design  but for a lot of clients they get really  involved and  they want to bring their own images and  because interior design is so much more  based on more elusive things compared to  architecture  like they're more focused on the mood of  the space  i think there's a much higher chance of  misalignment and so i think this step is  a really really good check-in point and  i think it's really essential for  everyone just to make sure that we're  all on the same page once you have the  mood board you pull together the colors  which become the basis for your material  board each profession is really unique  and each designer and each office is  really distinct in how they operate as  an architect i learned so much from that  time when i was working with interior  designers and i think one of the most  eye-opening things was the relationships  that they were building with the clients  and  maybe it's a result of this more  intimate process where you talk for  prolonged periods of time about the  client's desires but i found that  interior designers  in most cases had a much more  personal relationship with their clients  and i just thought it was a really  important element that i could take into  my work as an architect i hope this  video gave you a little bit of insight  into the differences and the nuances of  each profession if you have any  questions or thoughts on the topic  please let me know in the comments and  if you guys found this video helpful  make sure to give me a thumbs up  and i will see you guys in the next  video  [Music]  you

